{0 Fetcher}

A {!Workero.Fetcher} sends a request to another Worker, to static assets, or to
a Durable Object. It is the type of a
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/service-bindings/}service binding}.

{1 Configure}

Declare the target Worker in [wrangler.toml]. [binding] is the name that you pass
to [of_env]:

{@toml[
[[services]]
binding = "BACKEND"
service = "backend-worker"
]}

Static assets have a binding too. Name it with [binding] under [[assets]]:

{@toml[
[assets]
directory = "./public"
binding = "ASSETS"
]}

{1 Use}

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let backend = Fetcher.of_env env "BACKEND" in
  match URL.path (Request.url req) with
  | "/users" ->
      let res =
        Fetcher.fetch ~method_:POST
          ~headers:[ ("content-type", "application/json") ]
          ~body:{|{"name":"Ada"}|} backend "http://backend/users"
      in
      Response.text ~status:(`Status (Response.status res)) (Response.body_text res)
  | _ -> Fetcher.fetch_request backend req

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- {!Workero.Fetcher.of_env} opens the binding. It raises [Invalid_argument] when
  there is no such binding, or when the binding has no [fetch] method.
- {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch} sends a request and waits for the response headers.
  It takes the same optional arguments as {!Workero.fetch}. A [GET] or [HEAD]
  request with a body raises [Invalid_argument].
- {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch_request} forwards a {!Workero.Request.t} as it is. The
  runtime consumes the body of the request. Use {!Workero.Request.clone} first
  if the handler reads it too.
- {!Workero.Entrypoint.call} calls an RPC method of a binding that names an
  entrypoint class. See {{!page-entrypoints}Entrypoints}.

The URL must be absolute. A service binding ignores the host, so write any
host, such as [http://backend/users]. The target Worker sees the path and the
query. The target receives the request in its [fetch] handler.

If the target raises, the call raises {!Workero.Js_error}. A target that answers
with a status such as 404 or 500 is not an error: read the status with
{!Workero.Response.status}.

{1 Test locally}

Run the target Worker in the same [wrangler dev] session by passing several
configuration files:

{@sh[
npx wrangler dev -c wrangler.toml -c ../backend/wrangler.toml
]}

The tests of workero run against a [service] binding in [workerd]. They do not
cover an assets binding, which has the same [fetch] method.
